{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-36343" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find a vector equation of the normal line to the surface $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}-{y}^{2}={z}^{2}$ at the point $(2,0,1).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067198/state.json" "file_sha256" "99704f3b48f88db4fd7ca174fb2eab96166a8f624c2c49908f1bc18899b8b71b"} {"question" "q-36343" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}-{y}^{2} & ={z}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}-{y}^{2}-{z}^{2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y,z)=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}-{y}^{2}-{z}^{2}$, our surface can be viewed as the level surface $f(x,y,z)=0.$ \n\nA normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point $(2,0,1)$ can be found by computing $∇f(2,0,1).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(2,0,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}-{y}^{2}-{z}^{2})i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}-{y}^{2}-{z}^{2})j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}-{y}^{2}-{z}^{2})k \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{2}\\text{ }i-2y\\text{ }j-2z\\text{ }k \\\\ ∇f(2,0,1) & =\\frac{(2)}{2}\\,i-2(0)\\text{ }j-2(1)\\,k \\\\  & =i-2\\,k \\\\  & =⟨1,0,-2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the vector equation of the line that is parallel to $∇f(2,0,1)$ and passes through the point $(2,0,1)$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =⟨2,0,1⟩+t\\,∇f(2,0,1)=⟨2,0,1⟩+t\\,⟨1,0,-2⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067198/state.json" "file_sha256" "99704f3b48f88db4fd7ca174fb2eab96166a8f624c2c49908f1bc18899b8b71b"} {"question" "q-36343" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067198/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d96b737c97a40fd0f87c577b0dd6e5ffe6dd8330cd73d45241abc17a55f3a95e"} {"question" "q-36343" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067198/state.json" "file_sha256" "99704f3b48f88db4fd7ca174fb2eab96166a8f624c2c49908f1bc18899b8b71b"} {"question" "q-36343" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "r=⟨1,0,-2⟩+t\\,⟨1,0,-2⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] ["math" "r=⟨1,0,-2⟩+t\\,⟨2,0,1⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] ["math" "r=⟨2,0,1⟩+t\\,⟨1,0,-2⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] ["math" "r=⟨2,0,1⟩+t\\,⟨2,0,1⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] ["math" "r=⟨2,0,1⟩+t\\,⟨2,1,-1⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067198/state.json" "file_sha256" "99704f3b48f88db4fd7ca174fb2eab96166a8f624c2c49908f1bc18899b8b71b"} {"question" "q-36343" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "r=⟨2,0,1⟩+t\\,⟨1,0,-2⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067198/state.json" "file_sha256" "99704f3b48f88db4fd7ca174fb2eab96166a8f624c2c49908f1bc18899b8b71b"} {"question" "q-37570" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "For the function $f(x,y,z)=xy+5x+{z}^{2}$, find a vector that's perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(1,-1,2).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067198/state.json" "file_sha256" "99704f3b48f88db4fd7ca174fb2eab96166a8f624c2c49908f1bc18899b8b71b"} {"question" "q-37570" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(1,-1,2)$ can be found by computing \n$∇f(1,-1,2).$ \n\nSo, we first find the gradient and evaluate it at $(x,y,z)=(1,-1,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(xy+5x+{z}^{2})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(xy+5x+{z}^{2})\\,j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(xy+5x+{z}^{2})\\,k \\\\  & =(y+5)\\text{ }i+x\\text{ }j+2z\\text{ }k \\\\ ∇f(1,-1,2) & =(-1+5)\\,i+(1)\\,j+2(2)\\,k \\\\  & =4\\,i+j+4\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $4\\,i+j+4\\,k$ is perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(1,-1,2).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067198/state.json" "file_sha256" "99704f3b48f88db4fd7ca174fb2eab96166a8f624c2c49908f1bc18899b8b71b"} {"question" "q-37570" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067198/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d96b737c97a40fd0f87c577b0dd6e5ffe6dd8330cd73d45241abc17a55f3a95e"} {"question" "q-37570" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067198/state.json" "file_sha256" "99704f3b48f88db4fd7ca174fb2eab96166a8f624c2c49908f1bc18899b8b71b"} {"question" "q-37570" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-4\\,i-2\\,k"] ["math" "4\\,i+j+4\\,k"] ["math" "4\\,i-2\\,j+4\\,k"] ["math" "4\\,j+k"] ["math" "i+2\\,j+4\\,k"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067198/state.json" "file_sha256" "99704f3b48f88db4fd7ca174fb2eab96166a8f624c2c49908f1bc18899b8b71b"} {"question" "q-37570" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "4\\,i+j+4\\,k"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067198/state.json" "file_sha256" "99704f3b48f88db4fd7ca174fb2eab96166a8f624c2c49908f1bc18899b8b71b"} {"question" "q-71390" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the plane curve ${y}^{2}+3{x}^{2}=4xy.$  Find the equation of the normal line to the curve at the point $(-1,-3).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067198/state.json" "file_sha256" "99704f3b48f88db4fd7ca174fb2eab96166a8f624c2c49908f1bc18899b8b71b"} {"question" "q-71390" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}+3{x}^{2} & =4xy \\\\ {y}^{2}+3{x}^{2}-4xy & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)={y}^{2}+3{x}^{2}-4xy$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=0.$  \n\nA normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(-1,-3)$ can be found by computing $∇f(-1,-3).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(-1,-3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({y}^{2}+3{x}^{2}-4xy)\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2}+3{x}^{2}-4xy)\\,j \\\\  & =(6x-4y)\\,i+(2y-4x)\\,j \\\\ ∇f(-1,-3) & =(6(-1)-4(-3))\\,i+(2(-3)-4(-1))\\,j \\\\  & =6\\,i-2\\,j \\\\  & =⟨6,-2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $∇f(-1,-3)=⟨6,-2⟩$ is a normal vector to the curve at $(-1,-3).$\n\nFinally, the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $v=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y}⟩$ and passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}} & =\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}} \\\\ \\frac{x-(-1)}{6} & =\\frac{y-(-3)}{-2} \\\\ (-2)(x+1) & =6(y+3) \\\\ -2x-2 & =6y+18 \\\\ 6y+2x+20 & =0 \\\\ 3y+x+10 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067198/state.json" "file_sha256" "99704f3b48f88db4fd7ca174fb2eab96166a8f624c2c49908f1bc18899b8b71b"} {"question" "q-71390" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067198/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d96b737c97a40fd0f87c577b0dd6e5ffe6dd8330cd73d45241abc17a55f3a95e"} {"question" "q-71390" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067198/state.json" "file_sha256" "99704f3b48f88db4fd7ca174fb2eab96166a8f624c2c49908f1bc18899b8b71b"} {"question" "q-71390" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "3y+2x+11=0"] ["math" "3y+x+10=0"] ["math" "3y-x+8=0"] ["math" "y+3x+20=0"] ["math" "y+3x+4=0"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067198/state.json" "file_sha256" "99704f3b48f88db4fd7ca174fb2eab96166a8f624c2c49908f1bc18899b8b71b"} {"question" "q-71390" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "3y+x+10=0"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067198/state.json" "file_sha256" "99704f3b48f88db4fd7ca174fb2eab96166a8f624c2c49908f1bc18899b8b71b"} {"question" "q-71399" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "For the function $f(x,y,z)={e}^{x+y+z}+x+y+z$, find a vector that's perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(0,0,0).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067198/state.json" "file_sha256" "99704f3b48f88db4fd7ca174fb2eab96166a8f624c2c49908f1bc18899b8b71b"} {"question" "q-71399" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(0,0,0)$ can be found by computing \n$∇f(0,0,0).$ \n\nSo, we first find the gradient and evaluate it at $(x,y,z)=(0,0,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{x+y+z}+x+y+z)\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{x+y+z}+x+y+z)\\,j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({e}^{x+y+z}+x+y+z)\\,k \\\\  & =({e}^{x+y+z}+1)\\text{ }i+({e}^{x+y+z}+1)\\text{ }j+({e}^{x+y+z}+1)\\text{ }k \\\\ ∇f(0,0,0) & =({e}^{0+0+0}+1)\\,i+({e}^{0+0+0}+1)\\,j+({e}^{0+0+0}+1)\\,k \\\\  & =2\\,i+2\\,j+2\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $2\\,i+2\\,j+2\\,k$ is perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(0,0,0).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067198/state.json" "file_sha256" "99704f3b48f88db4fd7ca174fb2eab96166a8f624c2c49908f1bc18899b8b71b"} {"question" "q-71399" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067198/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d96b737c97a40fd0f87c577b0dd6e5ffe6dd8330cd73d45241abc17a55f3a95e"} {"question" "q-71399" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067198/state.json" "file_sha256" "99704f3b48f88db4fd7ca174fb2eab96166a8f624c2c49908f1bc18899b8b71b"} {"question" "q-71399" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2\\,i+2\\,j+2\\,k"] ["math" "2\\,i+2\\,j+k"] ["math" "2\\,i+j+2\\,k"] ["math" "2\\,i+j+k"] ["math" "i+2\\,j+2\\,k"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067198/state.json" "file_sha256" "99704f3b48f88db4fd7ca174fb2eab96166a8f624c2c49908f1bc18899b8b71b"} {"question" "q-71399" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2\\,i+2\\,j+2\\,k"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067198/state.json" "file_sha256" "99704f3b48f88db4fd7ca174fb2eab96166a8f624c2c49908f1bc18899b8b71b"} {"question" "q-130723" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the plane curve $4xy+2{x}^{2}=x+2y.$  Find the equation of the normal line to the curve at the point $(2,-1).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067198/state.json" "file_sha256" "99704f3b48f88db4fd7ca174fb2eab96166a8f624c2c49908f1bc18899b8b71b"} {"question" "q-130723" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we bring all variables to one side: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4xy+2{x}^{2} & =x+2y \\\\ 4xy+2{x}^{2}-x-2y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)=4xy+2{x}^{2}-x-2y$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=0.$   \n\nA normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(2,-1)$ can be found by computing $∇f(2,-1).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(2,-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4xy+2{x}^{2}-x-2y)\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4xy+2{x}^{2}-x-2y)\\,j \\\\  & =(4y+4x-1)\\,i+(4x-2)\\,j \\\\ ∇f(2,-1) & =(4(-1)+4(2)-1)\\,i+(4(2)-2)\\,j \\\\  & =3\\,i+6\\,j \\\\  & =⟨3,6⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n Hence, $∇f(2,-1)=⟨3,6⟩$ is a normal vector to the curve at $(2,-1).$\n\nFinally, the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $v=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y}⟩$ and passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be written as: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}} & =\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}} \\\\ \\frac{x-2}{3} & =\\frac{y-(-1)}{6} \\\\ 6(x-2) & =3(y+1) \\\\ 2(x-2) & =y+1 \\\\ 2x-4 & =y+1 \\\\ 2x-y-5 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067198/state.json" "file_sha256" "99704f3b48f88db4fd7ca174fb2eab96166a8f624c2c49908f1bc18899b8b71b"} {"question" "q-130723" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067198/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d96b737c97a40fd0f87c577b0dd6e5ffe6dd8330cd73d45241abc17a55f3a95e"} {"question" "q-130723" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067198/state.json" "file_sha256" "99704f3b48f88db4fd7ca174fb2eab96166a8f624c2c49908f1bc18899b8b71b"} {"question" "q-130723" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2x-y-5=0"] ["math" "x+2y=0"] ["math" "x+y-1=0"] ["math" "x-2y-4=0"] ["math" "x-3y-5=0"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067198/state.json" "file_sh